\begin_layout Standard
Engineers get taught a version of Fourier series in which 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{n}:=\frac{1}{L}\int_{-L}^{L}f(x)\cos\frac{n\pi x}{L}dx$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $b_{n}:=\frac{1}{L}\int_{-L}^{L}f(x)\sin\frac{n\pi x}{L}dx$
\end_inset

,
 and then we can express a periodic function as 
\begin_inset Formula $f=\frac{a_{0}}{2}+\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\left(a_{n}\cos\frac{n\pi x}{L}+b_{n}\sin\frac{n\pi x}{L}\right)$
\end_inset

.
 We are spiritually superior to engineers,
 so we don't bother with this sine and cosine nonsense,
 which are shadows concealing the true nature of the divine.
 Rather,
 given 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

,
 we define (assuming period 
\begin_inset Formula $2\pi$
\end_inset

 for now) 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{f}(k):=\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}f(\theta)\exp(-ik\theta)\:d\theta$
\end_inset

,
 and then our series is 
\begin_inset Formula $f(x)=\sum_{k=-\infty}^{\infty}\fhat(k)\exp(ik\theta)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If a function were uniquely pinned down by its Fourier coëfficients,
 then we could say:
 if 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $g$
\end_inset

 have the same coëfficients,
 then 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $g$
\end_inset

 are equal.
 Then consider the difference 
\begin_inset Formula $h:=f-g$
\end_inset

.
 Putting both antecedent and consequent in terms of 
\begin_inset Formula $h$
\end_inset

:
 if 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall k\ \hat{h}(k)=0$
\end_inset

,
 then 
\begin_inset Formula $h=0$
\end_inset

.
 That's not quite true,
 because a function can differ at finitely many points and still have the same integral and therefore the same Fourier coëfficients,
 but it's spiritually true:
 
\begin_inset Formula $h(\theta_{0})=0$
\end_inset

 when 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 is continuous at 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{0}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We should do some exercises.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Ch1#2
\series default
.
 
\series bold
a
\series default
.
 
\emph on
Proposition
\emph default
.
 
\begin_inset Formula $f(\theta)\sim\fhat(0)+\sum_{k\ge1}\left(\fhat(k)+\fhat(-k)\right)\cos n\theta+i\left(\fhat(k)-\fhat(-k)\right)\sin n\theta$
\end_inset

,
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\emph on
Proof
\emph default
.
 This is intuitive enough that writing out the algebra shouldn't be difficult at all.
 We have 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{k=-\infty}^{\infty}\fhat(k)\exp(ik\theta)=\sum_{k=-\infty}^{\infty}\fhat(k)\left(\cos(k\theta)+i\sin(k\theta)\right)=\sum_{k=-\infty}^{\infty}\fhat(k)\cos(k\theta)+i\fhat(k)\sin(k\theta)$
\end_inset

.
 Then we'll separate the negative,
 positive,
 and zero parts of the integer argument.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
So we have 
\begin_inset Formula $\fhat(0)\left(\cancelto{1}{\cos(0)}+\cancel{\sin(0)}\right)+\sum_{k\ge1}\fhat(-k)\cos(-k\theta)+i\fhat(-k)\sin(-k\theta)+\sum_{k\ge1}\fhat(k)\cos(k\theta)+i\fhat(k)\sin(k\theta)$
\end_inset

 and then cosine is even and sine is odd,
 so that becomes 
\begin_inset Formula $\fhat(0)+\sum_{k\ge1}\fhat(-k)\cos(k\theta)-i\fhat(-k)\sin(k\theta)+\sum_{k\ge1}\fhat(k)\cos(k\theta)+i\fhat(k)\sin(k\theta)$
\end_inset

 which is 
\emph on
quod erat demonstrandum
\emph default
 after regrouping.
 
\begin_inset Formula $\checkmark$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
b
\series default
.
 
\emph on
Proposition
\emph default
.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 is even,
 then 
\begin_inset Formula $\fhat(k)=\fhat(-k)$
\end_inset

 and we have a cosine series.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\emph on
Attempt
\emph default
.
 Suppose 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 is even.
 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{f}(k):=\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}f(\theta)\exp(-ik\theta)\:d\theta$
\end_inset

.
 Can we integrate by parts (
\begin_inset Formula $\int u\,dv=uv-\int v\,du$
\end_inset

) even if we don't non-symbolically know the derivative of 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

?
 Presumably it doesn't matter:
 the point is that it should be the same if we swap 
\begin_inset Formula $k\leftrightarrow-k$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
First,
 I tried assigning 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}\underbrace{f(\theta)}_{u:=}\underbrace{\exp(-ik\theta)}_{dv:=}\:d\theta$
\end_inset

,
 yielding 
\begin_inset Formula $du:=f'(\theta)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $v:=\frac{-1}{ik}\exp(-ik\theta)$
\end_inset

.
 Then after a long calculation (now omitted from the current revision of this document) I realized I was going in circles.
 (The boundary term was going to be zero again,
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\cancel{-}\frac{\cancel{-}1}{\cancel{ik}}\cancel{ik}=1$
\end_inset

.) Showing that 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}f(\theta)\exp(-ik\theta)\:d\theta$
\end_inset

 equals itself doesn't help us at all.
 Then I thought I should be doing the calculation for 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{f}(-k)$
\end_inset

 (
\emph on
not
\emph default
 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{f}(k)$
\end_inset

),
 which also (omitted) was circular:
 in retrospect 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

integrate by parts,
 then integrate by parts again `the other way'
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 was not going to be helpful no matter where we start from.
 There has to be some other trick.
 But what?
 The solution somehow needs to depend on 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 being even—
and we did use that fact in determining that the boundary term vanishes.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Claude Opus 5 says that we should actually be doing a change-of-variables like 
\begin_inset Formula $\varphi:=-\theta$
\end_inset

 here (the changes in the limits should cancel with 
\begin_inset Formula $d\varphi=-d\theta$
\end_inset

 and only the integrand gets negated,
 which gets absorbed by evenness),
 and that,
 more generally,
 when the hypothesis is a symmetry statement,
 that's the transformation to apply.
 Also,
 we're not even allowed to use parts like that because we don't know that 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 is differentiable.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let's do that.
 We have 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{f}(k):=\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}f(\theta)\exp(-ik\theta)\:d\theta$
\end_inset

,
 and we substitute 
\begin_inset Formula $\varphi:=-\theta$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $d\varphi=-d\theta$
\end_inset

 to get 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{f}(k)=\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{\pi}^{-\pi}-f(-\varphi)\exp(ik\varphi)\,d\varphi=\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}f(\varphi)\exp(ik\varphi)\,d\varphi$
\end_inset

,
 so 
\begin_inset Formula $\fhat(-k)=\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}f(\varphi)\exp(-ik\varphi)\,d\varphi=\fhat(k)$
\end_inset

.
 
\begin_inset Formula $\checkmark$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The remaining question is,
 why is that a cosine series?
 That comes from part a.
 We have as a proposition that 
\begin_inset Formula $f(\theta)\sim\fhat(0)+\sum_{k\ge1}\left(\fhat(k)+\fhat(-k)\right)\cos n\theta+i\left(\fhat(k)-\fhat(-k)\right)\sin n\theta$
\end_inset

.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $\fhat(k)=\fhat(-k)$
\end_inset

,
 that becomes 
\begin_inset Formula $\fhat(0)+\sum_{k\ge1}2\fhat(k)\cos n\theta+i\cancel{\left(\fhat(k)-\fhat(k)\right)}\sin n\theta$
\end_inset

.
 
\begin_inset Formula $\checkmark$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
c
\series default
.
 
\emph on
Proposition
\emph default
.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 is odd,
 then 
\begin_inset Formula $\fhat(k)=-\fhat(-k)$
\end_inset

 and we have a sine series.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\emph on
Proof
\emph default
.
 Now that we've been straightened out from being retarded,
 it's presumably the same deal.
 The 
\begin_inset Formula $\varphi=-\theta$
\end_inset

 substitution again brings us to 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{f}(k)=\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{\pi}^{-\pi}-f(-\varphi)\exp(ik\varphi)\,d\varphi$
\end_inset

,
 but this time 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 is odd,
 so we get 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}-f(\varphi)\exp(ik\varphi)\,d\varphi$
\end_inset

,
 so 
\begin_inset Formula $\fhat(-k)=\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}-f(\varphi)\exp(-ik\varphi)\,d\varphi=-\fhat(k)$
\end_inset

,
 and thus 
\begin_inset Formula $\fhat(k)=-\fhat(-k)$
\end_inset

,
 which is a sine series because 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{k\ge1}\left(\fhat(k)+\fhat(-k)\right)\cos n\theta+i\left(\fhat(k)-\fhat(-k)\right)\sin n\theta$
\end_inset

 becomes 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{k\ge1}\cancel{\left(\fhat(k)-\fhat(k)\right)}\cos n\theta+2i\fhat(k)\sin n\theta$
\end_inset

.
 
\begin_inset Formula $\checkmark$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
d
\series default
.
 
\emph on
Proposition
\emph default
.
 Suppose that for 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta\in\R$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $f(\theta+\pi)=f(\theta)$
\end_inset

.
 Then 
\begin_inset Formula $\fhat(k)=0$
\end_inset

 for odd 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\emph on
Proof
\emph default
.
 We were advised that when the hypothesis is a symmetry statement,
 that's the transformation to apply,
 so for lack of any better ideas,
 we'll try substituting 
\begin_inset Formula $\varphi:=\theta+\pi$
\end_inset

,
 which implies 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta=\varphi-\pi$
\end_inset

.
 (Claude Opus 5 says I made a sign error and a bounds error on an earlier attempt,
 so let's do it again.) 
\begin_inset Formula $\fhat(k)=\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}f(\theta)\exp(-ik\theta)\,d\theta=\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{0}^{2\pi}f(\varphi-\pi)\exp(-ik(\varphi-\pi))\,d\varphi=\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{0}^{2\pi}f(\varphi-\pi)\exp(ik\pi-ik\varphi))\,d\varphi=\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{0}^{2\pi}\underbrace{f(\varphi)}_{=f(\varphi-\pi)}\exp(ik\pi)\exp(-ik\varphi)\,d\varphi$
\end_inset

.
 For odd 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\exp(ik\pi)=-1$
\end_inset

.
 So we have 
\begin_inset Formula $\fhat(k)=-\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{0}^{2\pi}f(\varphi)\exp(-ik\varphi)\,d\varphi=-\fhat(k)$
\end_inset

,
 so 
\begin_inset Formula $\fhat(k)=0$
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
e
\series default
.
 
\emph on
Proposition
\emph default
.
 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 is real-valued if and only if 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall k\ \overline{\fhat(k)}=\fhat(-k)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\emph on
Proof
\emph default
.
 (
\begin_inset Formula $\Rightarrow$
\end_inset

) Suppose 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 is real-valued.
 Then 
\begin_inset Formula $\overline{\fhat(k)}=\overline{\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}f(\theta)\exp(-ik\theta)\:d\theta}=\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}\overline{f(\theta)}\overline{\exp(-ik\theta)}\:d\theta=\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}f(\theta)\exp(ik\theta)\:d\theta=\fhat(-k)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
(
\begin_inset Formula $\Leftarrow$
\end_inset

) Suppose 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall k\ \overline{\fhat(k)}=\fhat(-k)$
\end_inset

.
 That is to say,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\overline{\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}f(\theta)\exp(-ik\theta)\:d\theta}=\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}f(\theta)\exp(ik\theta)\:d\theta$
\end_inset

.
 We can pull the overline into the integral like 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}\overline{f(\theta)}\overline{\exp(-ik\theta)}\:d\theta$
\end_inset

,
 and into the exponential like 
\begin_inset Formula $\overline{\exp(-ik\theta)}=\exp(ik\theta)$
\end_inset

.
 (We know that 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

 is real,
 so 
\begin_inset Formula $\overline{\theta}=\theta$
\end_inset

.) So we have 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}\overline{f(\theta)}\exp(ik\theta)\:d\theta=\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}f(\theta)\exp(ik\theta)\:d\theta$
\end_inset

.
 It's tempting to just declare that this implies
\begin_inset Formula $\overline{f(\theta)}=f(\theta)$
\end_inset

 and therefore that 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 is real-valued,
 but I'm worried that that's a sloppy inference even if it has to be spiritually true.
 For example,
 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 being non-real at a single point wouldn't affect the value of the integral.
 I'm daydreaming that there should be an elegant proof that if 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 is continuous,
 then it can only be non-real-valued on an interval,
 not a single point,
 and then there would have to be some 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 that exposes it?
 But we don't even know that 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 is continuous,
 only Riemann-integrable.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Good sign that I'm not retarded:
 Prof.
 Lai actually addressed this concern in class on Monday 31 August!
 He said that we could assume that 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 is continuous (or if it's not continuous,
 then it's real-valued merely almost everywhere).
 (Is the implication that the venerable Stein and Shakarchi made a mistake in an exercise this simple?)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We're probably not quite done with the proof.
 Suppose that 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 is continuous,
 and we have 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}\overline{f(\theta)}\exp(ik\theta)\:d\theta=\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}f(\theta)\exp(ik\theta)\:d\theta$
\end_inset

.
 Suppose for a contradiction that 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 is not real-valued:
 there exists nonempty 
\begin_inset Formula $B\subseteq[-\pi,\pi]$
\end_inset

 for which 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in B\rightarrow f(x)\notin\R$
\end_inset

 (and possibly empty 
\begin_inset Formula $A\subseteq[-\pi,\pi]$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in A\rightarrow f(x)\in\R$
\end_inset

,
 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $A\cup B=[-\pi,\pi]$
\end_inset

).
 Then the integral over 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 is the same for both the 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\overline{f}$
\end_inset

 integrands,
 but the integral over 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 is different,
 which implies that the integral over 
\begin_inset Formula $A\cup B=[-\pi,\pi]$
\end_inset

 is different.
 To see why the integral over 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 is different,
 consider that
\begin_inset Formula $\int_{B}\overline{f(\theta)}\exp(ik\theta)\:d\theta$
\end_inset

 is the limit of a Riemann sum 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum\text{\overline{f(\theta)}\exp(ik\theta)\ensuremath{\cdot}\ensuremath{\Delta}\ensuremath{\theta}}$
\end_inset

,
 while 
\begin_inset Formula $\int_{B}f(\theta)\exp(ik\theta)\:d\theta$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum f(\theta)\exp(ik\theta)\cdot\ensuremath{\Delta}\ensuremath{\theta}$
\end_inset

.
 What I mean is,
 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{k}+b_{k}i\ne a_{k}-b_{k}i$
\end_inset

 when 
\begin_inset Formula $b_{k}\ne0$
\end_inset

,
 and they're still not going to be equal when you multiply both sides by the same thing (
\emph on
e.g.

\emph default
 
\begin_inset Formula $\exp(ik\theta)\ensuremath{\cdot}\ensuremath{\Delta}\ensuremath{\theta}$
\end_inset

),
 and they're still not going to be equal when you sum over all 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{k}b_{k}i\cdot C_{k}\ne\sum_{k}-b_{k}i\cdot C_{k}$
\end_inset

.
 I feel like this should be pretty obvious even if I'm not explaining it elegantly.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Claude Opus 5 isn't buying it.
 Rather,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}\overline{f(\theta)}\exp(ik\theta)\:d\theta=\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}f(\theta)\exp(ik\theta)\:d\theta$
\end_inset

 is saying that 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\overline{f}$
\end_inset

 have the same Fourier coëfficients,
 which makes them equal everywhere that they're continuous (what Stein and Shakarchi call Theorem 2.1).
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Ch2#4
\series default
.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $f(\theta):=\theta(\pi-\theta)$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
b
\series default
.
 
\emph on
Proposition
\emph default
.
 
\begin_inset Formula $f(\theta)=\frac{8}{\pi}\sum_{k\in\mathbb{N}\mathrm{\,odd}}\frac{\sin k\theta}{k^{3}}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\emph on
First attempt
\emph default
.
 
\begin_inset Formula $\fhat(k)=\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{0}^{2\pi}(\pi\theta-\theta^{2})\exp(-ik\theta)=\frac{1}{2\pi}\cdot\left(\pi\int_{0}^{2\pi}\theta\exp(-ik\theta)-\int_{0}^{2\pi}\theta^{2}\exp(-ik\theta)\right)$
\end_inset

.
 Then I guess this is just going to be some tedious integrations by parts?
 (Then I waded into a huge mess of calculations,
 now omitted from this document,
 that didn't get to where I needed to go.)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\emph on
Guidance
\emph default
.
 Claude Opus 5 points out that the function was defined on 
\begin_inset Formula $[0,\pi]$
\end_inset

;
 it's the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

odd
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 extension of that.
 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}f(\theta)\exp(-ik\theta)\,d\theta=\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}f(\theta)\left(\cos k\theta-i\sin k\theta\right)\,d\theta$
\end_inset

 but odd-function times even-function is odd (function 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

parity
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 rules follow integer 
\emph on
addition
\emph default
 parity) so only the sine part will be left:
 
\begin_inset Formula $-\frac{i}{2\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}f(\theta)\sin k\theta\,d\theta$
\end_inset

.
 Opus 5 points out that 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 is odd 
\emph on
by stipulation
\emph default
 (the expression 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta(\pi-\theta)$
\end_inset

 defined everywhere isn't odd),
 so we have odd-function times odd-function is even,
