\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Ch2#10
\series default
.
 
\emph on
Theorem
\emph default
.
 Suppose 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 is periodic with period 
\begin_inset Formula $2\pi$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset

 times continuously differentiable.
 Then 
\begin_inset Formula $\fhat(k)=O\left(\frac{1}{\left|k\right|^{m}}\right)$
\end_inset

 as 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|k\right|\to\infty$
\end_inset

,
 which is to say that there exists a constant 
\begin_inset Formula $C$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|\fhat(k)\right|\le\frac{C}{\left|k\right|^{m}}$
\end_inset

,
 which is to say that there exists a bound 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|k\right|^{m}\left|\fhat(k)\right|\le B$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\emph on
Comment
\emph default
.
 We get a hint to integrate by parts (which is now legal,
 since we know that 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 is differentiable (
\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset

 times continuously).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\emph on
Proof
\emph default
.
 Assigning 
\begin_inset Formula $\fhat(k)=\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}\underbrace{f(\theta)}_{u}\underbrace{\exp(-ik\theta)}_{dv}\,d\theta$
\end_inset

 yields 
\begin_inset Formula $du:=f'(\theta)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $v:=\frac{-1}{ik}\exp(-ik\theta)$
\end_inset

,
 such that we have 
\begin_inset Formula $\left.\frac{-1}{ik}f(\theta)\exp(-ik\theta)\right|_{\theta:=-\pi}^{\pi}+\frac{1}{ik}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}f'(\theta)\exp(-ik\theta)\,d\theta$
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula $=\frac{-1}{ik}f(\pi)\exp(-ik\pi)+\frac{1}{ik}f(-\pi)\exp(ik\pi)+\frac{1}{ik}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}f'(\theta)\exp(-ik\theta)\,d\theta$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Because 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 is periodic with period 
\begin_inset Formula $2\pi$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $f(-\pi)=f(\pi)$
\end_inset

,
 so the boundary terms become 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{i}{k}f(\pi)\exp(-ik\pi)-\frac{i}{k}f(\pi)\exp(ik\pi)=f(\pi)\frac{i}{k}\cdot\left(\exp(-ik\pi)-\exp(ik\pi)\right)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
So far we have 
\begin_inset Formula $\fhat(k)=\frac{1}{2\pi}\cdot\frac{i}{k}\cdot\left(f(\pi)\cdot\left(\exp(-ik\pi)-\exp(ik\pi)\right)-\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}f'(\theta)\exp(-ik\theta)\,d\theta\right)$
\end_inset

.
 And then,
 what,
 we integrate by parts again (we can go to 
\begin_inset Formula $f^{(m)}$
\end_inset

),
 and get another factor of 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{i}{k}$
\end_inset

?
 But that wouldn't affect the boundary terms from the first integration-by-parts.
 Halp?!
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Claude Opus 5 offers the hint that we can do more to evaluate 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{i}{k}\cdot f(\pi)\cdot\left(\exp(-ik\pi)-\exp(ik\pi)\right)$
\end_inset

 at successive integers.
 That is,
 for 
\begin_inset Formula $k:=1$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $e^{-\pi i}-e^{\pi i}=-1-(-1)=0$
\end_inset

.
 For 
\begin_inset Formula $k:=2$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $e^{-2\pi i}-e^{2\pi i}=1-1=0$
\end_inset

.
 The difference 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 half-turns forward and backward will always be zero,
 so the boundary term does cancel.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
That gives us 
\begin_inset Formula $\fhat(k)=-\frac{1}{2\pi}\cdot\frac{i}{k}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}f'(\theta)\exp(-ik\theta)\,d\theta$
\end_inset

 (Opus 5 pointed out that I forgot the factor of 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{1}{ik}$
\end_inset

 here),
 which is to say that 
\begin_inset Formula $\fhat(k)=\frac{1}{ik}\hat{f'}(k)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
But then integrating 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{f'}(k)=\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}\underbrace{f'(\theta)}_{u}\underbrace{\exp(-ik\theta)}_{dv}\,d\theta$
\end_inset

 is going to go the same:
 it's going to be 
\begin_inset Formula $\left.\frac{-1}{ik}f'(\theta)\exp(-ik\theta)\right|_{\theta:=-\pi}^{\pi}+\frac{1}{ik}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}f''(\theta)\exp(-ik\theta)\,d\theta$
\end_inset

.
 The boundary term is going to vanish again.
 (A periodic function has a periodic derivative.) 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
So it would seem that 
\begin_inset Formula $\fhat(k)=\frac{1}{ik}\hat{f'}(k)=-\frac{1}{k^{2}}\hat{f''}(k)$
\end_inset

 
\emph on
&c
\emph default
.
 But derivatives only came into it because we used parts;
 we're actually trying to bound 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|\fhat(k)\right|$
\end_inset

.
 We have 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|\fhat(k)\right|=\frac{1}{\left|k\right|^{m}}\left|\hat{f^{(m)}(k)}\right|$
\end_inset

 (and 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{f^{(m)}}$
\end_inset

 has to exist because the function is 
\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset

 times continuously differentiable).
 So the qualitative moral here is that if a function is 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset

-any
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 times continuously differentiable,
 then the Fourier coëfficients have to go to zero like an 
\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset

th power,
 which means that for smooth functions,
 it's decreasing faster than any fixed power.
 So smooth functions can't have too much weight on sufficiently high-frequency components?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Opus 5 points out that we didn't finish getting a bound:
 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|\hat{f^{(m)}(k)}\right|$
\end_inset

 isn't constant in 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

.
 But if 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{k}\hat{f^{(m)}}\exp(ik\theta)$
\end_inset

 converges,
 then its coëfficients have to go to zero,
 so there will be a bound.
 Opus 5 objects that we don't know that the series converges (this point in the curriculum is doing a lot of work on that question!) and gets us started on the actual bound:
 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|\hat{f^{(m)}(k)}\right|=\left|\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}f^{(m)}(\theta)\exp(-ik\theta)\,d\theta\right|\le\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}\left|f^{(m)}(\theta)\right|\underbrace{\left|\exp(-ik\theta)\right|}_{=1}\,d\theta\le\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}\left|f^{(m)}(\theta)\right|\,d\theta$
\end_inset

 but since 
\begin_inset Formula $f^{(m)}$
\end_inset

 iis continuous on the compact interval 
\begin_inset Formula $[-\pi,\pi]$
\end_inset

,
 it must be bounded by the extreme value theorem.
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In lecture on 2 September,
 we got an introduction to the Dirac delta and 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

good kernels.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 We're interested in the convergence properties of the partial sum 
\begin_inset Formula $S_{N}f(x)=\sum_{k=-N}^{N}\fhat(k)\exp(ikx)=\sum_{k=-N}^{N}\underbrace{\left(\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}f(y)\exp(-iky)\:dy\right)}_{\fhat(k)}\exp(ikx)$
\end_inset

.
 (I'm introducing 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 so that we don't mix up 
\begin_inset Formula $\fhat(k)$
\end_inset

 variable of integration with the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

outer
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

.) Uniform convergence lets us switch the sum and the integral sign:
 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}\sum_{k=-N}^{N}f(y)\exp(-iky)\:dy\exp(ikx)=\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}f(y)\sum_{k=-N}^{N}\exp(ik(x-y))\:dy$
\end_inset

.
 We can define the Dirichlet kernel as 
\begin_inset Formula $D_{N}(t):=\sum_{k=-N}^{N}\exp(ikt)$
\end_inset

,
 so we get 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}f(y)D_{N}(x-y)\,dy$
\end_inset

,
 which is actually a convolution 
\begin_inset Formula $(f*D_{N})(x)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Admittedly,
 it's not immediately clear how this helps us,
 because we are not yet a good friend of the convolution operation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Convolution with the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

one
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 function is an average:
 
\begin_inset Formula $(f*\boldsymbol{1})(x)=\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}f(y)\,dy$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Suppose we want to convolve with something to get the value at a point.
 We can use the indicator function on a narrow interval but scaled to integrate to one,
 and take the limit as the interval gets infinitely narrow.
 That's Dirac's delta.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\emph on
Proposition
\emph default
.
 For uniformly continuous 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\lim_{N\to\infty}\frac{N}{2}\int_{x-\frac{1}{N}}^{x+\frac{1}{N}}f(t)\,dt=f(x)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\emph on
Proof
\emph default
.
 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|\frac{N}{2}\int_{x-\frac{1}{N}}^{x+\frac{1}{N}}f(t)\,dt-f(x)\right|=\left|\frac{N}{2}\int_{x-\frac{1}{N}}^{x+\frac{1}{N}}f(t)\,dt-f(x)\cdot\underbrace{\frac{N}{2}\int_{x-\frac{1}{N}}^{x+\frac{1}{N}}1\,dt}_{\mathrm{``fancy\,one"}}\right|=\left|\frac{N}{2}\int_{x-\frac{1}{N}}^{x+\frac{1}{N}}f(t)\,dt-\frac{N}{2}\int_{x-\frac{1}{N}}^{x+\frac{1}{N}}f(x)\,dt\right|$
\end_inset

 so the linearity of integration gets us to 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|\frac{N}{2}\int_{x-\frac{1}{N}}^{x+\frac{1}{N}}f(t)-f(x)\,dt\right|$
\end_inset

.
 Then 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{N}{2}\left|\int_{x-\frac{1}{N}}^{x+\frac{1}{N}}f(t)-f(x)\,dt\right|\le\frac{N}{2}\int_{x-\frac{1}{N}}^{x+\frac{1}{N}}\left|f(t)-f(x)\right|dt$
\end_inset

.
 (The absolute value of an integral is not greater than the integral of the absolute value,
 because positive and negative parts of the integral can cancel.) Uniform continuity means that for any 
\begin_inset Formula $\varepsilon$
\end_inset

,
 there exists an 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{\varepsilon}$
\end_inset

 such that if 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|t-x\right|<\frac{1}{N_{\varepsilon}}$
\end_inset

,
 then 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|f(t)-f(x)\right|<\varepsilon$
\end_inset

.
 So for any 
\begin_inset Formula $\varepsilon$
\end_inset

,
 we can choose an 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{N}{2}\int_{x-\frac{1}{N}}^{x+\frac{1}{N}}\left|f(t)-f(x)\right|dt<\frac{N}{2}\int_{x-\frac{1}{N}}^{x+\frac{1}{N}}\varepsilon\,dt=\cancel{\frac{N}{2}\cdot\frac{2}{N}}\cdot\varepsilon=\varepsilon$
\end_inset

,
 which is 
\emph on
quod erat demonstrandum
\emph default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The significance of the proposition is that we've shown that 
\begin_inset Formula $\lim_{N\to\infty}f*\delta_{N}(x)=f(x)$
\end_inset

.
 More generally,
 we're interested in 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

good kernels
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula $K_{n}$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $\lim_{N\to\infty}f*K_{N}=f(x)$
\end_inset

,
 which satisfy three conditions.
 It turns out that the Dirichlet kernel is not 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

good
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
I borrowed Adrian Constantin's 
\emph on
Fourier Analysis:
