\end_inset

 and then apparently there's a half-angle formula such that we have 
\begin_inset Formula $2\cdot2\sin^{2}(\frac{x}{2})=4\sin^{2}(\frac{x}{2})$
\end_inset

,
 so 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|1-\exp(ix)\right|=2\left|\sin(\frac{x}{2})\right|$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
At this point I am consumed with despair that I couldn't realistically have learned Fourier analysis before 2024 (when LLMs started to become useful math tutors);
 none of these steps are incomprehensible once explained,
 but there's no way I could have realistically known to pull a half-angle identity out of a hat.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The reason we endured all this suffering was to get a bound independent of 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

:
 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|\sum_{k=1}^{N}\sin(kx)\right|\le\frac{2}{2\sin(\frac{\delta}{2})}=\frac{1}{\sin(\frac{\delta}{2})}$
\end_inset

.
 (Because 
\begin_inset Formula $x\ge\delta$
\end_inset

,
 so 
\begin_inset Formula $\sin(\frac{x}{2})\ge\sin(\frac{\delta}{2})$
\end_inset

,
 and the inequality goes the other way when it's in the denominator.)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
I didn't see how this would help us with our estimate of 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|\sum_{k=1}^{N}\frac{\sin kx}{k}-\frac{\pi}{2}-\frac{x}{2}\right|$
\end_inset

,
 but GPT-5.6 Sol points out that we're only asked to prove that the Fourier series converges,
 not that it converges to 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

,
 so it's tempting to just say 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Dirichlet's test with 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{k}:=\frac{1}{k}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $b_{k}:=\sin kx$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

,
 but GPT-5.6 Sol furthermore points out that that's not good enough for uniform convergence.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
(With some GPT-5.6 Sol tutoring on how to cannabalize the summation-by-parts estimate and some bookkeeping issues—
) We want to show that the partial sum 
\begin_inset Formula $S_{N}f(x)$
\end_inset

 converges uniformly on 
\begin_inset Formula $(-\pi,-\delta]\cup[\delta,\pi)$
\end_inset

.
 Hopefully without loss of generality,
 let's just think about 
\begin_inset Formula $[\delta,\pi)$
\end_inset

 for now.
 That means that for all 
\begin_inset Formula $\varepsilon$
\end_inset

 there exists an 
\begin_inset Formula $M_{\varepsilon}$
\end_inset

 such that for 
\begin_inset Formula $N\ge M\ge M_{\varepsilon}$
\end_inset

 and all 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in[\delta,\pi)$
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|S_{N}f(x)-S_{M-1}f(x)\right|<\varepsilon$
\end_inset

.
 (It's uniform because 
\begin_inset Formula $M$
\end_inset

 doesn't depend on 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

,
 and we're going down to 
\begin_inset Formula $M-1$
\end_inset

,
 so that we can write the resulting tail in sigma notation as
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{k=M}^{N}$
\end_inset

.) So we have 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|\sum_{k=M}^{N}\frac{1}{k}\cdot\sin kx\right|$
\end_inset

.
 From our derivation of Dirichlet's test above,
 we know that if 
\begin_inset Formula $\{a_{k}\}\downarrow0$
\end_inset

 and any partial sum of 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum c_{k}$
\end_inset

is bounded by 
\begin_inset Formula $C$
\end_inset

,
 then 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|\sum_{k=M}^{N}a_{k}c_{k+1}\right|\le3Ca_{M}$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We assign 
\begin_inset Formula $\underbrace{\frac{1}{k}}_{:=a_{k}}\cdot\underbrace{\sin kx}_{:=c_{k+1}}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $C:=\frac{1}{\sin(\frac{\delta}{2})}$
\end_inset

.
 Fix 
\begin_inset Formula $\varepsilon$
\end_inset

.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $M_{\varepsilon}:=\left\lceil \frac{3}{\varepsilon\sin(\frac{\delta}{2})}\right\rceil +1$
\end_inset

.
 Then for 
\begin_inset Formula $N\ge M\ge M_{\varepsilon},$
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|\sum_{k=M}^{N}\frac{1}{k}\sin kx\right|<\frac{\cancel{3}}{\cancel{\sin(\frac{\delta}{2})}}\cdot\frac{\varepsilon\cancel{\sin(\frac{\delta}{2})}}{\cancel{3}}=\varepsilon$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

