\begin_layout Standard

\emph on
Proof
\emph default
.
 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{1}{2i}\sum_{k\ne0}\frac{\exp(ikx)}{k}=\frac{1}{2i}\sum_{k\ne0}\frac{\exp(ik0)}{k}=\frac{1}{2i}\sum_{k\ne0}\frac{1}{k}$
\end_inset

 ...
 and I was thinking that doesn't converge,
 but GPT-5.6 Sol points out that we want the limit of the symmetric partial sum.
 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{k\ne0}\frac{1}{k}=\lim_{N\to\infty}\sum_{k=-N}^{-1}\frac{1}{k}+\sum_{k=1}^{N}\frac{1}{k}=\sum_{k=1}^{N}\frac{1}{k}-\frac{1}{k}=0$
\end_inset

.
