\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
HW2#3
\series default
 
\series bold
(Ch2#9)
\series default
.
 Consider the characteristic function 
\begin_inset Formula $\chi_{[a,b]}(x)=\begin{cases}
1 & x\in[a,b]\\
0 & \mathrm{else}
\end{cases}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
a
\series default
.
 
\emph on
Proposition
\emph default
.
 The Fourier series of 
\begin_inset Formula $\chi_{[a,b]}$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{b-a}{2\pi}+\sum_{k\ne0}\frac{\exp(-ika)-\exp(-ikb)}{2\pi ik}\exp(ikx)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\emph on
Proof
\emph default
.
 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}\chi_{[a,b]}(x)\exp(-ikx)\,dx=\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{a}^{b}\exp(-ikx)\,dx$
\end_inset

.
 When 
\begin_inset Formula $k:=0$
\end_inset

,
 that's 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{a}^{b}\exp(0)=\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{a}^{b}1\,dx=\frac{b-a}{2\pi}$
\end_inset

,
 otherwise it's 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{1}{2\pi}\cdot\frac{1}{-ik}\left.\exp(-ikx)\right|_{x=a}^{b}=\frac{1}{2\pi ik}\cdot\left(\exp(-ika)-\exp(ikb)\right)$
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
b
\series default
.
 
\emph on
Proposition
\emph default
.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $a\ne-\pi$
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $b\ne\pi$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $a\ne b$
\end_inset

,
 the Fourier series does not converge absolutely for any 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\emph on
Proof
\emph default
.
 (We got a series of walkthrough hints from Prof.
 Lai.) We're showing that 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{k\ne0}\left|\frac{\exp(-ika)-\exp(-ikb)}{2\pi ik}\exp(ikx)\right|$
\end_inset

 diverges.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{0}:=\frac{b-a}{2}$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The hint sequence has us proceed with 
\begin_inset Formula $\sin(k\theta_{0})$
\end_inset

,
 and I wasn't sure where the 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 went.
 GPT-5.6 Sol reminded me that 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|\exp(ikx)\right|$
\end_inset

 is one—
I note that the absolute value bars eliminating such factors has been a recurring theme that I shouldn't need to ask about again—
and explained that the choice of 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{0}$
\end_inset

 is to make the exponents symmetrical so that we can invoke the complex sine formula.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
That is,
 we see that 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{0}$
\end_inset

 is half the length of the interval.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset

 is the midpoint,
 then 
\begin_inset Formula $a=m-\theta_{0}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $b=m+\theta_{0}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We have
\begin_inset Formula $\left|\frac{\exp(-ika)-\exp(-ikb)}{2\pi ik}\exp(ikx)\right|=\frac{1}{\pi k}\left|\frac{\exp(-ika)-\exp(-ikb)}{2i}\right|=\frac{1}{\pi k}\left|\frac{\exp(-ik(m-\theta_{0}))-\exp(-ik(m+\theta_{0}))}{2i}\right|$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $=\frac{1}{\pi k}\cancelto{1}{\left|\exp(-im)\right|}\left|\frac{\exp(k\theta_{0})-\exp(-k\theta_{0})}{2i}\right|=\frac{1}{\pi k}\left|\sin(k\theta_{0})\right|$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
(With much more tutoring from GPT-5.6 Sol,
 on top of the hint sequence—
) We're trying to show that 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{k}\frac{1}{\pi k}\left|\sin(k\theta_{0})\right|$
\end_inset

 diverges.
 Consider some small 
\begin_inset Formula $c$
\end_inset

,
 and consider the interval of such 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|\sin x\right|\ge c$
\end_inset

:
 that's 
\begin_inset Formula $[\arcsin c,\pi-\arcsin c]$
\end_inset

.
 The length of that interval is 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi-\arcsin c-\arcsin c=\pi-2\arcsin c$
\end_inset

.
 Recall that the terms of our series are
\begin_inset Formula $\left|\sin(k\theta_{0})\right|$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $k\in\N$
\end_inset

:
 we're 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

sampling
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 the sine function at intervals of 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{0}$
\end_inset

.
 If the length of the interval where 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|\sin x\right|\ge c$
\end_inset

 is longer than 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

,
 then every such interval will contain at least one of the sample points.
 The intervals are the same size,
 so the not-less-than-
\begin_inset Formula $c$
\end_inset

 terms can't thin out faster than linearly,
 so 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{k}\frac{1}{\pi k}\left|\sin(k\theta_{0})\right|$
\end_inset

 has a subseries that 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

looks like
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{1}{\pi}\sum_{k}\frac{c}{k}$
\end_inset

,
 which diverges as the harmonic series.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Specifically,
 the 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

th 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|\sin\right|$
\end_inset

-not-less-than-
\begin_inset Formula $c$
\end_inset

 interval 
\begin_inset Formula $[j\pi+\arcsin c,(j+1)\pi-\arcsin c]$
\end_inset

 contains a point 
\begin_inset Formula $n_{j}\theta_{0}<(j+1)\pi-\arcsin c$
\end_inset

,
 so 
\begin_inset Formula $n_{j}<\frac{(j+1)\pi-\arcsin c}{\theta_{0}}$
\end_inset

,
 so 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{1}{n_{j}}>\frac{\theta_{0}}{(j+1)\pi-\arcsin c}$
\end_inset

,
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{\theta_{0}}{(j+1)\pi-\arcsin c}>\frac{\theta_{0}}{(j+1)\pi}$
\end_inset

 (subtracting 
\begin_inset Formula $\arcsin c$
\end_inset

 in the denominator makes the denominator smaller,
 which makes the fraction bigger).
 We were concerned with the series 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{k}\frac{1}{\pi k}\left|\sin(k\theta_{0})\right|$
\end_inset

.
 Taking the terms corresponding to our 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|\sin\right|$
\end_inset

-not-less-than-
\begin_inset Formula $c$
\end_inset

 intervals (which we index with 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

),
 we have 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{j}\frac{1}{\pi n_{j}}\left|\sin(n_{j}\theta_{0})\right|\ge\sum_{j}\frac{\theta_{0}}{(j+1)\pi^{2}}c$
\end_inset

,
 which diverges as the harmonic series.
\end_layout

