\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
HW2#4 (Ch2#11)
\series default
.
 
\emph on
Theorem
\emph default
.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\{f_{j}\}_{j=1}^{\infty}$
\end_inset

 be a sequence of Riemann integrable functions on 
\begin_inset Formula $[0,1]$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $\lim_{j\to\infty}\int_{0}^{1}\text{\left|f_{j}(x)-f(x)\right|}=0$
\end_inset

.
 Then 
\begin_inset Formula $\lim_{j\to\infty}\hat{f_{j}}(k)=\fhat(k)$
\end_inset

 (uniformly).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\emph on
Proof
\emph default
.
 We want to show that for all 
\begin_inset Formula $\varepsilon$
\end_inset

,
 there exists a 
\begin_inset Formula $J$
\end_inset

 such that if 
\begin_inset Formula $j\ge J$
\end_inset

,
 then for all 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\left|\int_{0}^{1}f_{j}(x)\exp(-2\pi ikx)\,dx-\int_{0}^{1}f(x)\exp(-2\pi ikx)\,dx\right|<\varepsilon
\]

\end_inset

(It's uniform because 
\begin_inset Formula $J$
\end_inset

 doesn't depend on 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

.)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
And we know as a hypothesis that for all 
\begin_inset Formula $\varepsilon$
\end_inset

,
 there exists a 
\begin_inset Formula $J_{0}$
\end_inset

 such that if 
\begin_inset Formula $j\ge J_{0}$
\end_inset

,
 then 
\begin_inset Formula $\int_{0}^{1}\text{\left|f_{j}(x)-f(x)\right|}<\varepsilon$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Fix 
\begin_inset Formula $\varepsilon$
\end_inset

,
 and suppose 
\begin_inset Formula $j\ge J_{0}$
\end_inset

.
 We can state 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|\hat{f_{j}}(k)-\fhat(k)\right|$
\end_inset

 as 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|\int_{0}^{1}f_{j}(x)\exp(-2\pi ikx)-f(x)\exp(-2\pi ikx)\,dx\right|$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
By the linearity of integration and triangle inequality for integrals,
 we have 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\left|\int_{0}^{1}f_{j}(x)\exp(-2\pi ikx)-f(x)\exp(-2\pi ikx)\,dx\right|=\left|\int_{0}^{1}\exp(-2\pi ikx)\left(f_{j}(x)-f(x)\right)\,dx\right|\le\int_{0}^{1}\left|\exp(-2\pi ikx)\right|\left|f_{j}(x)-f(x)\right|\,dx
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
But 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|\exp(-2\pi ikx)\right|=1$
\end_inset

.
 So 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\left|\int_{0}^{1}f_{j}(x)\exp(-2\pi ikx)\,dx-\int_{0}^{1}f(x)\exp(-2\pi ikx)\,dx\right|\le\int_{0}^{1}\left|f_{j}(x)-f(x)\right|\,dx<\varepsilon
\]

\end_inset

which is 
\emph on
quod erat demonstrandum
\emph default
.
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let's think more about good kernels and the Fejer kernel.
 The three criteria for a good kernel are:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
It integrates to unity.
 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall k\,\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}K_{k}(\theta)\,d\theta=1$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
The integral of its absolute value is bounded:
 
\begin_inset Formula $\exists B\,\forall k\ \int_{-\pi}^{\pi}\left|K_{k}(\theta)\right|d\theta<B$
\end_inset

.
 That means,
 the way you get the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

integrates to one
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 behavior is not through infinite oscillations canceling each other out.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
It vanishes away from the origin:
 
\begin_inset Formula $\lim_{k\to\infty}\forall\delta\:\int_{[-\pi,-\delta]\cup[\delta,\pi]}K_{k}(\theta)\,d\theta=0$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We recall that the sum of a geometric series is 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{a}{1-r}$
\end_inset

 (for initial term 
\begin_inset Formula $a$
\end_inset

 and successive ratio 
\begin_inset Formula $r<1$
\end_inset

),
 but we should also recall the partial sum 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}ar^{k}=a\frac{1-r^{n}}{1-r}$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A standard derivation:
 
\begin_inset Formula $S:=\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}ar^{k}$
\end_inset

 implies 
\begin_inset Formula $rS=\sum_{k=1}^{n}ar^{k}$
\end_inset

.
 Then 
\begin_inset Formula $S-rS=a\cancel{r^{0}}-ar^{n}=a(1-r^{n})$
\end_inset

.
 But then 
\begin_inset Formula $S-rS=S(1-r)$
\end_inset

.
 So if 
\begin_inset Formula $S(1-r)=a(1-r^{n})$
\end_inset

,
 then 
\begin_inset Formula $S=a\frac{1-r^{n}}{1-r}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The Dirichlet kernel is 
\begin_inset Formula $D_{N}:=\sum_{k=-N}^{N}\exp(ik\theta)=\frac{\sin((N+\frac{1}{2})\theta)}{\sin(\frac{\theta}{2})}$
\end_inset

.
 Now 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{k=0}^{N}e^{ik\theta}$
\end_inset

 is a geometric series where the first term is 
\begin_inset Formula $e^{0}=1$
\end_inset

 and the ratio of terms is 
\begin_inset Formula $e^{i\theta}$
\end_inset

.
 (For 
\begin_inset Formula $e^{i\theta}\cdot e^{i\theta}=\exp(i\theta+i\theta)=\exp(2i\theta)$
\end_inset

,
 
\emph on
&c
\emph default
.) So the formula says the sum is 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{1-\exp(i\theta)^{N+1}}{1-\exp(i\theta)}$
\end_inset

.
 That was just the positive 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

.
 In the other direction,
 we have a series that starts at 
\begin_inset Formula $e^{-i\theta}$
\end_inset

 and and the ratio is also 
\begin_inset Formula $e^{-i\theta}$
\end_inset

,
 so the formula says 
\begin_inset Formula $e^{-i\theta}\frac{1-\exp(-i\theta)^{N}}{1-\exp(-i\theta)}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

